    "listLessons:[]": {
      "ok": true,
      "value": {
        "lessons": [
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "storageKey": "197133/dc48a09e-d9bb-420a-83d7-72dc2304c034",
                "streamLibraryId": "197133",
                "streamVideoId": "dc48a09e-d9bb-420a-83d7-72dc2304c034",
                "type": "video"
              },
              {
                "html": "<h3>Bunny Stream video player</h3><p>This lesson shows a real Bunny Stream video embedded in the platform player. Together delivers video through Bunny Stream, a global video host with adaptive streaming quality (HLS).</p><p>To add your own recording, upload it to your Bunny Stream library and paste the library and video IDs into the lesson editor.</p>",
                "type": "html"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:05.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 4,
            "id": "lesson-js-demo-video",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Video demo (Bunny Stream)",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/W6NZfCO5SIk",
                "type": "embed"
              },
              {
                "html": "<h3>Variables in JavaScript</h3><p>JavaScript declares variables with <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, and the older <code>var</code> keyword. Modern code mostly uses the first two.</p><ul><li><strong>const</strong> - a binding you do not intend to reassign.</li><li><strong>let</strong> - a variable whose value will change over time.</li></ul><p>Use <code>const</code> by default and switch to <code>let</code> when you need to assign a new value.</p>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "description": "MDN - JavaScript grammar and types",
                "type": "link",
                "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Grammar_and_types"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:06.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 12,
            "id": "lesson-js-variables-1",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Declaring variables",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "html": "<h3>Data types</h3><p>JavaScript distinguishes primitive types (<code>string</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>boolean</code>, <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>symbol</code>, and <code>bigint</code>) from objects, including arrays.</p><p>Primitive values are copied directly. With objects, the copied value is a reference to the same object, so changing that object is visible through either reference.</p>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/hdI2bqOjy3c",
                "type": "embed"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:07.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 15,
            "id": "lesson-js-variables-2",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Primitive and complex types",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/N8ap4k_1QEQ",
                "type": "embed"
              },
              {
                "html": "<h3>Functions</h3><p>A function is a piece of code you can call repeatedly. JavaScript functions are <strong>first-class values</strong>: you can assign them to variables and pass them as arguments.</p><ul><li>Function declaration: <code>function sum(a, b) { return a + b; }</code></li><li>Arrow function: <code>const sum = (a, b) =&gt; a + b;</code></li></ul>",
                "type": "html"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:08.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 18,
            "id": "lesson-js-functions-1",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Functions and arguments",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "html": "<h3>Closures</h3><p>A closure lets a function retain access to variables from the scope where it was created. This is one of the core mechanisms of the language.</p><p>Closures are useful for counters, helper functions, and functional programming.</p>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "description": "You Don’t Know JS - a free JavaScript book series",
                "type": "link",
                "url": "https://github.com/getify/You-Dont-Know-JS"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:09.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 22,
            "id": "lesson-js-functions-2",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Closures and scope",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/0ik6X4DJKCc",
                "type": "embed"
              },
              {
                "html": "<h3>The DOM tree</h3><p>The DOM (Document Object Model) represents a page as a tree of nodes. Select elements with <code>querySelector</code> and <code>querySelectorAll</code>.</p><ul><li><code>document.querySelector(&quot;.example&quot;)</code> - the first matching element.</li><li><code>document.querySelectorAll(&quot;li&quot;)</code> - a list of all matching elements.</li></ul>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "name": "DOM methods cheat sheet (PDF)",
                "pdfUrl": "/assets/sample-lesson.pdf",
                "type": "pdf"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:10.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 14,
            "id": "lesson-js-dom-1",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Selecting DOM elements",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "html": "<h3>Handling events</h3><p>Make a page interactive by listening for events with <code>addEventListener</code>. Common events include <code>click</code>, <code>input</code>, and <code>submit</code>.</p><p>When handling a form, call <code>event.preventDefault()</code> if you need to take control of the default browser behavior.</p>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/jS4aFq5-91M",
                "type": "embed"
              },
              {
                "description": "MDN — addEventListener",
                "type": "link",
                "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventTarget/addEventListener"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:11.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 19,
            "id": "lesson-js-dom-2",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Events and interaction",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/8dWL3wF_OMw",
                "type": "embed"
              },
              {
                "html": "<h3>Final project</h3><p>Put your new skills together by building a simple to-do app. You will use DOM manipulation, event handling, and <code>localStorage</code> to save state.</p><ul><li>Add and remove tasks.</li><li>Mark tasks as complete.</li><li>Keep your data after refreshing the page.</li></ul>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "description": "Project starter repository on GitHub",
                "type": "link",
                "url": "https://github.com/acme-courses/todo-vanilla"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:12.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 35,
            "id": "lesson-js-project-1",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Project: a to-do list",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/SqcY0GlETPk",
                "type": "embed"
              },
              {
                "html": "<h3>JSX syntax</h3><p>JSX lets you describe an interface with HTML-like syntax directly in JavaScript. A JSX compiler turns this markup into JavaScript calls that create React elements. The classic transform uses <code>React.createElement</code>.</p><p>Each component returns one element tree. To group sibling elements without an extra wrapper, use a fragment: <code>&lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt;</code>.</p>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "description": "React documentation - writing markup with JSX",
                "type": "link",
                "url": "https://react.dev/learn/writing-markup-with-jsx"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:13.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 11,
            "id": "lesson-react-jsx-1",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "What is JSX?",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "html": "<h3>Components</h3><p>A component is a function that returns JSX. Pass data into components through <strong>props</strong>, which are read-only inputs.</p><ul><li>Start component names with a capital letter.</li><li>Props are immutable: a component should never modify them.</li></ul>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/Rh3tobg7hEo",
                "type": "embed"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:14.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 16,
            "id": "lesson-react-jsx-2",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Components and props",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/O6P86uwfdR0",
                "type": "embed"
              },
              {
                "html": "<h3>The useState hook</h3><p>State is data that changes during the lifetime of a component. The <code>useState</code> hook returns a pair: the current value and a setter function.</p><p>Updating state triggers another render of the component with the new data.</p>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "description": "React documentation - useState",
                "type": "link",
                "url": "https://react.dev/reference/react/useState"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:15.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 14,
            "id": "lesson-react-state-1",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Component state with useState",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "html": "<h3>Lifting state up</h3><p>When several components need the same data, move that state into their closest shared parent and pass it down through props. This is a basic pattern for data flow in React.</p>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/bMknfKXIFA8",
                "type": "embed"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:16.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 17,
            "id": "lesson-react-state-2",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Data flow and lifting state up",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/0ZJgIjIuY7U",
                "type": "embed"
              },
              {
                "html": "<h3>The useEffect hook</h3><p>Use <code>useEffect</code> to synchronize with external systems, such as network requests, subscriptions, or manual DOM updates. The dependency array controls when the effect runs again.</p><ul><li>An empty array <code>[]</code> runs the effect after mounting; development Strict Mode also checks setup and cleanup with an extra cycle.</li><li>Return a cleanup function to release resources before the effect runs again or the component unmounts.</li></ul>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "description": "React documentation - useEffect",
                "type": "link",
                "url": "https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:17.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 21,
            "id": "lesson-react-hooks-1",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Side effects with useEffect",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          },
          {
            "contents": [
              {
                "html": "<h3>Custom hooks</h3><p>Extract reusable stateful logic into custom hooks: functions whose names start with <code>use</code>. This lets components share logic without duplicating code.</p>",
                "type": "html"
              },
              {
                "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/6ThXsUwLWvc",
                "type": "embed"
              },
              {
                "description": "react-use - a library of ready-made hooks (GitHub)",
                "type": "link",
                "url": "https://github.com/streamich/react-use"
              }
            ],
            "createdAt": "2026-07-01T12:00:18.000Z",
            "durationMinutes": 25,
            "id": "lesson-react-hooks-2",
            "isPreview": false,
            "legacyId": null,
            "name": "Creating custom hooks",
            "tenantId": "tenant-studio"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
